- id: '2094277998145094001'
  text: "The common mistake is opening a coding agent and asking:\n\n\"Build a modern\
    \ landing page for my product\"\n\nThat usually produces the same gradients, rounded\
    \ cards, oversized headings, and random animations.\n\nHere is the workflow I\
    \ use instead.\n\n1 - Check the architecture first\n\nBefore touching the UI,\
    \ understand the product:\n\n- Who is it for?\n- What action should users take?\n\
    - What pages and routes exist?\n- What content and data are available?\n-Which\
    \ components already exist?\n- Which components already exist? design respect?\n\
    \nA beautiful interface built around the wrong product structure is still a bad\
    \ interface.\n\n2 - Collect visual references\n\nFind 5 to 10 references from\
    \ websites, pictures, any kind of visuals/content you like\n\nFor each reference,\
    \ write down what you actually like:\n\n- Information hierarchy\n- Typography\n\
    - Color treatment\n- Image composition\n- Spacing\n- Navigation\n- Motion\n- Overall\
    \ mood\n\n\"Make a minimalistic website like Apple\" is too vague. \n\n\"Use large\
    \ editorial typography, restrained navigation, wide product photography, and hard\
    \ section transitions\" gives the agent something it can apply.\n\nIt can be a\
    \ bit boring to do at the beginning, but once everything is setup you'll thank\
    \ me.\n\n3 - Write DESIGN.md\n\nTurn your decisions into a file the coding agent\
    \ can read.\n\nMy DESIGN.md usually contains:\n\n- Product context and audience\n\
    - Main user action\n- Brand personality\n- Core visual idea\n- Design principles\n\
    - Color and typography tokens\n- Spacing rules\n- Page structure\n- Component\
    \ rules\n- Responsive behavior\n- Motion rules\n- Accessibility requirements\n\
    - Explicit anti-patterns\n\nThe anti-patterns matter.\nFor example:\n\n- No purple\
    \ AI gradients\n- No glassmorphism\n- No unnecessary cards\n- No pill-shaped container\
    \ around every label\n- No transition: all\n- No essential information hidden\
    \ behind hover\n\nThis prevents the model from falling back to its usual design\
    \ habits.\n\n4 - Use GPT-Image to explore directions\n\nBefore coding, generate\
    \ several visual concepts.\n\nI use a prompt like:\n\nCreate four distinct visual\
    \ directions for a landing page for [product]. Keep the same content hierarchy:\
    \ navigation, hero, social proof, features, process, and CTA. Explore editorial,\n\
    cinematic, minimal, and technical directions. Show a 1440px desktop layout. Focus\
    \ on typography, composition, color, and image treatment. Avoid generic SaaS cards,\
    \ purple gradients, glass effects, and decorative dashboards.\n\nKeep the product\
    \ and content consistent between generations. Change the art direction, not the\
    \ business.\n\nGPT-Image is useful here because comparing four images is faster\
    \ than building four React implementations.\n\n5 - Pick one direction and extract\
    \ its rules\n\nDo not send the image to the coding agent with \"build this.\"\n\
    \nExplain what should be copied:\n\n- Header height\n- Type scale\n- Content width\n\
    - Section rhythm\n- Image crop\n- Color balance\n- Divider treatment\n- Interaction\
    \ behavior\n\nUpdate DESIGN.md with those decisions.\n\nThe image is a reference.\
    \ DESIGN.md is the specification.\n\n6 - Build in passes\n\nI ask the agent to\
    \ work in this order:\n\n1. Page structure and semantic HTML\n2. Typography, colors,\
    \ and spacing\n3. Images and responsive crops\n4. Interactions and motion\n5.\
    \ Mobile behavior\n6. Accessibility and performance\n\nTrying to solve everything\
    \ in one prompt makes visual mistakes harder to diagnose.\n\n7 -Compare the rendered\
    \ page with the reference\n\nOpen the real page at desktop and mobile sizes.\n\
    \nThen check:\n\n- Is the hierarchy equally clear?\n- Are the proportions correct?\n\
    - Is the hero too empty or too busy?\n- Does the typography carry the design?\n\
    - Are sections visually connected?\n- Does mobile feel designed or merely stacked?\n\
    - Did the agent add components that were never requested?\n\nTake a screenshot\
    \ and give the agent a concrete correction list.\n\n8 - Iterate until the implementation\
    \ follows the system\n\nGood AI-assisted design comes from a clear feedback loop:\n\
    \nArchitecture → references → image concepts → DESIGN.md → implementation → screenshot\
    \ review → corrections.\n\nThe coding model can write the components quickly.\n\
    \nYour job is to give it a visual system, clear constraints, and specific feedback\
    \ before it starts inventing one for you.\n\n9 - Create a Skill inside Hermes\
    \ Agent to do this\n\nThis process can take a lot of time when repeated manually.\n\
    \nThe solution is to package each step into a focused skill:\n\n- architecture-review\
    \ reads the codebase, routes, components, and constraints.\n- reference-research\
    \ collects and analyzes visual references.\n- design-direction defines the visual\
    \ concept and anti-patterns.\n- image-ideation uses GPT-Image to generate several\
    \ directions.\n- design-md creates and maintains the design specification.\n-\
    \ frontend-build implements the selected direction.\n- visual-review compares\
    \ screenshots with the references.\n- accessibility-review checks contrast, keyboard\
    \ use, motion, and responsive behavior.\n\nEach skill should define:\n\n- Required\
    \ inputs\n- Steps to follow\n- Expected output\n- Constraints\n- Verification\
    \ method\n\nThen connect the skills into one workflow.\n\nInstead of explaining\
    \ the entire process for every project, you can tell the agent:\n\n\"Analyze this\
    \ product, propose four visual directions, create DESIGN.md, implement the selected\
    \ direction, review the rendered page, and fix the differences.\"\n\nThe agent\
    \ handles the repeated work. You keep control over the decisions that require\
    \ taste, especially the final visual direction."
